      "message": "This CL is already very large. Let\u0027s deal with this issue in a separate CL.\n\n---\n\nDetails:\n\ngit history shows that this check ultimately comes from the following old commit of yours:\n\n```\ncommit 1e4a14f41028fa7e0fd5d64af24f6957e534a972\nAuthor: fbarchard@google.com \u003cfbarchard@google.com\u003e\nDate:   Tue Mar 10 22:30:47 2015 +0000\n\n    scale avoid math overflow in fixed point for large images\n    BUG\u003d410\n    TESTED\u003dset LIBYUV_WIDTH\u003d65536 out\\release\\libyuv_unittest.exe --gtest_catch_exceptions\u003d0 --gtest_filter\u003dlibyuvTest.ScaleTo320x240_None\n    R\u003dharryjin@google.com\n    \n    Review URL: https://webrtc-codereview.appspot.com/42319004\n    \n    git-svn-id: http://libyuv.googlecode.com/svn/trunk@1320 16f28f9a-4ce2-e073-06de-1de4eb20be90\n```\n\nThis check comes up again in a recent CL of yours:\nhttps://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/libyuv/libyuv/+/7886217\n\nThis is all I know. I don\u0027t know whether the check should be \u003e 32768 or \u003e\u003d 32768. But based on the following comment added in the recent CL:\n\n```\n  // Reject dimensions larger than 32768 (or smaller than -32768 for height).\n  // This prevents FixedDiv signed integer overflows that can lead to division\n  // by zero/overflow crashes (SIGFPE on x86) or incorrect step calculations.\n```\n\nthe answer to your question is in the FixedDiv function.",
